# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
spinoza-ethics-2.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: spinoza-ethics-2.logic.r2 and spinoza-ethics-2.logic.r2.2 have "judges_by_emotion" and "imaginary_causes" and "uncertainty"; spinoza-ethics-2.logic.r2.3 has "judges_by_emotion" and "imaginary_causes" and "uncertainty" and "judges_by_emotion ∧ imaginary_causes → affected_self_cause"; they differ when only "a man often judges things solely by his emotions", "the things which a man believes cause pleasure or pain, and therefore endeavours to promote or prevent, are often purely imaginary" and "there is the uncertainty of things alluded to in III. xxviii." are true, which the first allows
spinoza-ethics-2.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps spinoza-ethics-2.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "a man often judges things solely by his emotions", "the things which a man believes cause pleasure or pain, and therefore endeavours to promote or prevent, are often purely imaginary" and "there is the uncertainty of things alluded to in III. xxviii." are true and "a man may be at one time affected with pleasure, and at another with pain, accompanied by the idea of himself as cause" is false. Is S1, "Lastly, from this inconstancy in the nature of human judgment, inasmuch as a man often judges things solely by his emotions, and inasmuch as the things which he believes cause pleasure or pain, and therefore endeavours to promote or prevent, are often purely imaginary, not to speak of the uncertainty of things alluded to in III. xxviii.; we may readily conceive that a man may be at one time affected with pleasure, and at another with pain, accompanied by the idea of himself as cause.", true then?
spinoza-ethics-2.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: spinoza-ethics-2.logic.r2 has "judges_by_emotion ∧ imaginary_causes ∧ uncertainty → affected_self_cause" and "affected_self_cause ∧ repentance_def ∧ complacency_def → understand"; spinoza-ethics-2.logic.r2.2 has "judges_by_emotion ∧ imaginary_causes → affected_self_cause" and "affected_self_cause ∧ repentance_def ∧ complacency_def → understand"; they differ when only "a man often judges things solely by his emotions" and "the things which a man believes cause pleasure or pain, and therefore endeavours to promote or prevent, are often purely imaginary" are true, which the first allows; spinoza-ethics-2.logic.r2.3 has "affected_self_cause ∧ repentance_def ∧ complacency_def → understand"
